&ldquo;That clock's wrong again. That clock hardly ever knows what time it is;
      and when it does know, it lies about it&mdash;which amounts to the same
      thing. Alfred!&rdquo;

He turned the hands of his clock forward till they marked twenty-five
      minutes to one, and said, &ldquo;Now see if you can't keep right for a while&mdash;else
      I'll raffle you!&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, I won't, honor bright. But such weather, oh, such weather! You've got
      to keep your spirits up artificially. It is snowy, and blowy, and gusty,
      and bitter cold! How is the weather with you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Hang it, she would have introduced me to this angel in that sky-blue
      dressing-gown with red-hot lapels! Women never think, when they get
      a-going.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Twenty-five minutes to three! Nearly two hours, and I didn't believe it
      was two minutes! Is it possible that this clock is humbugging again? Miss
      Ethelton! Just one moment, please. Are you there yet?&rdquo;

The girl blushed again, murmured to herself, &ldquo;It's right down cruel of him
      to ask me!&rdquo; and then spoke up and answered with admirably counterfeited
      unconcern, &ldquo;Five minutes after eleven.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I&mdash;well, nothing in particular. It's very lonesome here. It's
      asking a great deal, I know, but would you mind talking with me again by
      and by&mdash;that is, if it will not trouble you too much?&rdquo;

&ldquo;There is no longer any question about it. She avoids me. She continually
      excuses herself. If I could see her, if I could speak to her only a moment&mdash;but
      this suspense&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, Alonzo, none that have gone before me knew what love was, none that
      come after me will ever know what happiness is. I float in a gorgeous
      cloud land, a boundless firmament of enchanted and bewildering ecstasy!&rdquo;

Servants were called by the elders, in both places. Unto one was given the
      order, &ldquo;Pile this fire high, with hickory wood, and bring me a
      roasting-hot lemonade.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Suppose,&rdquo; replied the Reverend, &ldquo;suppose that, instead of music that was
      passing along and being stolen, the burden of the wire was loving
      endearments of the most private and sacred nature?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, indeed! Of course, then, I misunderstood you, and I most humbly beg
      your pardon, ha-ha-ha! No doubt you said, 'Don't sing it any more
      to-day.'&rdquo;

&ldquo;Alonzo, dear, I have been wrong. You could not have said so cruel a
      thing. It must have been some one who imitated your voice in malice or in
      jest.&rdquo;

In the beginning of his pilgrimage he used often to say, &ldquo;Ah, if I could
      but hear the 'Sweet By-and-by'!&rdquo; But toward the end of it he used to shed
      tears of anguish and say, &ldquo;Ah, if I could but hear something else!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, thank Heaven, found at last! Speak to me, Rosannah, dearest! The
      cruel mystery has been unraveled; it was the villain Burley who mimicked
      my voice and wounded you with insolent speech!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh; Alonzo, stay by me! Leave me not for a moment! Let me feel that you
      are near me! Tell me we shall never be parted more! Oh, this happy hour,
      this blessed hour, this memorable hour!&rdquo;

&ldquo;We will make record of it, my Rosannah; every year, as this dear hour
      chimes from the clock, we will celebrate it with thanksgivings, all the
      years of our life.&rdquo;

There was a little pause. Then a diffident small voice replied, &ldquo;I blush&mdash;but
      it is with pleasure, it is with happiness. Would&mdash;would you like to
      have it soon?&rdquo;

&ldquo;So we be the happiest ones that that day's suit looks down upon in the
      whole broad expanse of the globe, why need we care? Call it the 1st of
      April, dear.&rdquo;
